{:edb/db-before-t 765 :edb/db-after-t 766 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534078 50 #inst "2026-10-06T02:42:36.204Z" 13194139534078 true] [17592186087443 1100 "Calculate $A^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534078 false] [17592186087443 1100 "Calculate $A^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534078 true] [17592186087443 1145 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534078 true] [17592186087443 1145 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -0 \\\\ - (-2) & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAA^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I_{2}✓ \\\\\nA^{-1}A &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I_{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534078 false] [17592186087444 1100 "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534078 true] [17592186087444 1100 "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534078 false] [17592186087444 1145 "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({A})=1 \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({C})=3 \\cdot 3 - (-3) \\cdot 3 = 18.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(C) \\neq 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular." 13194139534078 true] [17592186087444 1145 "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n$\\det({A})=1 \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 0$.\nBecause $\\det(A)=0$, we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n$\\det({C})=3 \\cdot 3 - (-3) \\cdot 3 = 18$.\nBecause $\\det(C) \\neq 0$, we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular." 13194139534078 false] [17592186087445 1100 "If\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 - k & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - k\\end{bmatrix}$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k$." 13194139534078 false] [17592186087445 1100 "If \n$[\\begin{aligned}1-k & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-k\\end{aligned}]$\n is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k.$" 13194139534078 true] [17592186087445 1145 "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0$. Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= (1 - k)(1 - k) - 4 \\\\\n&= k^{2} - 2k - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^{2} - 2k - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(k - 3)(k + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 3,-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1$." 13194139534078 false] [17592186087445 1145 "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(1-k)(1-k)-4 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-2k-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-2k-3 & =0 \\\\ (k-3)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =3,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1.$" 13194139534078 true] [17592186087638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277054 13194139534078 true] [17592186087638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277061 13194139534078 true] [17592186087638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277068 13194139534078 true] [17592186087638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277075 13194139534078 true] [17592186087638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277103 13194139534078 true] [17592186087639 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277040 13194139534078 true] [17592186087639 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277082 13194139534078 true] [17592186087639 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277089 13194139534078 true] [17592186087639 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277113 13194139534078 true] [1759